.TH ANAGRAM 6L 11-Sep-1987.SH NAMEanagram \- search dictionary for anagrams.SH SYNOPSIS.I anagram[ -d <dictfile> ] [ -m <Nr> ] [ -l ] [ -r ] string1 [ string2 . . . ].SH DESCRIPTIONFor each of its arguments,.I anagramsearches the dictionary file for words which contain the exact same letters asthe argument, but in any order, and writes them to the standard output.Dictionary entires which contain non-alphabetic characters are ignored..sp.I Anagramis useful for cheating on word-puzzles..spOptions are:.sp.in +10.ti -5.B -d.I <dictfile>\- Used to specify.I <dictfile>as the file containing the word list, instead of the default..sp.ti -5.B -m.I <Nr>\- Indicates the minimum length of words to be read from the dictionary file.This is useful for avoiding lots of anagrams constructed from tiny littleconnective words and abbreviations. The default minimum is two..sp.ti -5.B -l\- Allows.I anagramto find partial matches, printing out the leftover letters between squarebrackets. This is useful for finding "near matches" or for deciphering ascrambled multi-word phrase where one of the words isn't literally in the wordlist..sp.ti -5.B -r\- Recursive mode. This one is real useful. In this mode, when.I anagramfinds a match which doesn't use up all the letters in the argument string, ittries to match recursively on the remainder of the string. This allows it tounscramble an entire phrase into separate words. Be warned that.I anagramoperates rather slowly in this mode..in -10.SH EXAMPLES.nf.na\&% anagram stoppostspotstop.sp% anagram -l stopopt [s]Po [st]postpot [s]so [pt]sop [t]spotSt [op]stopto [ps]top [s].sp% anagram -m 3 -r tfyoowtrforty towforty two.fi.ad.SH AUTHORFred Blonder <fred@mimsy.umd.edu>.SH FILES.in +5.ti -5/usr/dict/words \- default dictionary file.br.ti -5/usr/dict/web2 \- a more complete dictionary, which takes longer to search..in -5.SH "SEE ALSO"grep(1).SH DIAGNOSTICSThe only likely errors are problems opening or reading the dictionary file..SH BUGSExtremely inefficient. Makes multiple passes through the dictionary file whenoperating in recursive mode.